# bmcd is a cargo package: Buildroot fetches the GitHub archive, runs
# `cargo vendor`, and stores the re-packed archive (the "-cargo2" suffix)
# -- so the sum covers the vendored crates too, exactly as Buildroot does
# for its own cargo packages.
#
# THE SUM DEPENDS ON THE RUST TOOLCHAIN, not just on the commit. The
# archive is named after the commit alone, so a stale one in dl/ looks
# right and hashes wrong. Measured on 2026-09-08 against commit 9b5cffa:
# rust-bin 1.85.0 vendored
# 9815f9cb190773112439b8979222f45ee79124f15ace2d29c5484141be679f65,
# rust-bin 1.98.1 vendored
# 84b9825371b085cea0f21ef03efd50893cfb16c0806cecb98c3700d605b44b9c.
# Recompute after any Rust bump, from a tree re-extracted AFTER the bump --
# buildroot_patches/ are applied at extract time, so a tree extracted
# before the patch changed still carries the old toolchain and produces the
# old sum under a name that looks correct.
#
# Computed with buildroot/local.mk REMOVED. A dev override rsyncs a working
# tree instead of downloading the pinned archive, so a hash computed with one
# in place would describe whatever happened to be on the workstation.
#
# The version is commit b68d0c2 on excavador-turing/bmcd branch `hive`,
# released as v2.34.0. Two releases in one pin:
#
# v2.33.0 -- a node's USB bus number is not part of its identity. The port
# check that stops a flash of node 2 writing node 1 compared the whole path,
# bus included, against a bus hardcoded to 1. This board pairs an OHCI and an
# EHCI controller as COMPANIONS for the same physical ports, so the bus a
# device lands on is decided by its speed: the same hub port is 1-1.2 for a
# full-speed device and 2-1.2 for a high-speed one. A Rockchip in maskrom is
# high-speed, so the daemon refused every RK1 it was asked to flash or expose
# as mass storage. Nothing on this board could be flashed until this release.
#
# v2.34.0 -- the daemon can list the microSD card: name, size, modified time,
# and whether each entry could be written to a node. Path confinement is one
# function with its own tests, because the same resolver will serve rename and
# delete later; root and target are both canonicalised, so `../..` and a
# symlink off the card are refused by the same rule.
#
# v2.35.0 -- a lost catalogue refresh no longer wedges the board, and the
# firmware listing survives a reboot.
#
# The claim that says "a refresh is running" was a flag taken before the work
# and released by a guard afterwards: correct for a panic, wrong for a task
# dropped before its first poll or a blocking join that never returns. Either
# way it stayed set and no later refresh could start. bmc-2 sat that way for
# three and a half hours on 2026-09-11, `refreshing: true` with nothing
# running at all. It is a deadline now, which cannot be lost.
#
# The listing is kept at /mnt/overlay/firmware-catalog.json, so the person who
# just flashed a board does not wait through a 75-230 s fan-out to see what it
# is running. Written only when the offering changes: the overlay is NAND and
# UBI reports five free eraseblocks of 2040 on this board.
#
# No listener change and no gate change in any of them.
#
# Measured 2026-09-12 with rust-bin 1.98.1 in the pinned container: `make
# bmcd-source` reported got bfb577e1 against a zeroed expectation, then re-run
# to confirm it fetches and verifies cleanly.
#
# v2.35.0 -> v2.36.0: who may reach this board, from the interface that asks
# for the password.
#
# Both halves of a board's access control were filesystem-only and arrived over
# SSH -- the local password through `passwd` on a console, the client CA
# through a script in another repository. `GET /api/bmc/access` reports the
# local account, the trust anchor with its fingerprint and expiry, the header
# an identity is read from and which of config/override/default chose it, and
# how the ASKING request was authenticated. `POST /access/password` and
# `PUT`/`DELETE /access/client-ca` change the two.
#
# Not through the legacy dispatcher, deliberately: every `opt=set` is written
# to the audit log with its whole query string, so a password routed that way
# would be recorded in clear. These take a JSON body.
#
# The daemon does not rewrite config.yaml -- it has no YAML writer and that
# file is the operator's. It owns two small files instead, and config.yaml
# still wins wherever it speaks. A consequence worth having: client_ca falls
# back to a fixed path when the FILE EXISTS, so a board whose anchor was
# removed comes back up rather than refusing to start.
#
# Measured 2026-09-12 with rust-bin 1.98.1 in the pinned container: `make
# bmcd-source` reported got 94e7dc72 against a zeroed expectation, then re-run
# on a cleaned tree to confirm it fetches and verifies cleanly.
#
# v2.36.0 -> v2.36.1: a refusal keeps the status it was built with.
#
# Found on bmc-2 within the hour of flashing v2.28.0, by asking the board the
# questions its new guards refuse. They all refused with the right message and
# all of them answered 500, because `impl ResponseError for LegacyResponse {}`
# was empty and actix's default applies to everything. Invisible until now:
# every other handler comes back through `Responder`, which has always
# honoured the carried status. 500 is the canonical retryable status, so a
# client told 500 for "that password is wrong" retries an answer that will
# never change.
#
# Measured 2026-09-12 with rust-bin 1.98.1 in the pinned container: `make
# bmcd-source` reported got 2fbb2956 against a zeroed expectation, then re-run
# on a cleaned tree to confirm it fetches and verifies cleanly.
